Practical 2.3 — DDL Operations

Aim:
To create database tables and perform DDL operations such as ALTER, RENAME, DROP, and TRUNCATE on database relations.

Objectives:
• Create Employee, Department, and Student tables.
• Modify table structures using ALTER commands.
• Rename tables and columns.
• Remove records and columns.

Theory:
DDL (Data Definition Language) commands are used
to create and modify the structure of database objects. 
Commands like CREATE, ALTER, RENAME, DROP, and TRUNCATE help manage database schema.
These commands define tables, change structures, and organize data efficiently.

Conclusion:
Successfully created tables and performed DDL operations on the database.






Practical 3.3 — Users, Roles and Transactions

Aim:
To create users and roles, assign privileges, and perform transaction control operations.

Objectives:
• Create users and roles.
• Grant and revoke permissions.
• Use transaction commands.
• Perform rollback and commit operations.

Theory:
Transaction Control Language (TCL) commands are 
used to manage database transactions. COMMIT saves 
changes permanently, ROLLBACK cancels changes, and SAVEPOINT
creates intermediate restore points. User privileges
and roles improve security and control access to database resources.

Conclusion:
Successfully implemented user access control and transaction management.





Practical 8 — Database Trigger

Aim:
To implement triggers for salary validation and restricting invalid operations.

Objectives:
• Create INSERT and UPDATE triggers.
• Validate employee salary.
• Prevent invalid deletion operations.

Theory:
Triggers are database objects that automatically 
execute in response to events such as INSERT, 
UPDATE, and DELETE. They help enforce business 
rules, maintain data integrity, and automate 
validation processes within the database.

Conclusion:
Successfully created triggers and validated employee operations.




Practical 10 — MongoDB Aggregation

Aim:
To perform aggregation operations using MongoDB employee collection.

Objectives:
• Insert employee documents.
• Apply grouping and filtering.
• Calculate salary statistics.
• Store results in new collections.

Theory:
MongoDB aggregation framework processes
documents through multiple stages called pipelines.
Operators such as $match, $group, $sum, $avg, $max, 
and $min are used to filter, group, and calculate results efficiently.

Conclusion:
Successfully performed aggregation operations and analyzed collection data.




